    "content": "Rental LED screens are built to be assembled, shown and taken down hundreds of times a year. That changes everything about how they are designed and how a crew works with them. This guide walks through a rental LED wall build from rigging to line check, explains what makes a touring cabinet fast and safe, covers curved and creative configurations, and lists the pre-show checks used on concert stages, conferences and outdoor events.\n\n## The build order of a rental LED wall\n\n1.   0 1 Rigging: header bars fly from the truss on certified points with a load calculation per point; ground-stacked walls get base frames, rear bracing and ballast.\n2.   0 2 Cabinets: 500 × 500 mm or 500 × 1000 mm rental panels hang from the header and lock to their neighbours with quarter-turn locks and alignment pins.\n3.   0 3 Power and data: each column is daisy-chained with a redundant data loop closed at the last cabinet and power distributed from a dimmer-city or distro.\n4.   0 4 Processing: the LED processor maps the wall, loads the cabinet calibration and locks to the show frame rate and the media server.\n5.   0 5 Line check: a test pattern walks every cabinet before content is loaded, and the wall is white-balanced to the lighting rig.\n\n## What makes a touring LED cabinet fast\n\nWeight and tooling. A 500 × 1000 mm cabinet under 25 kg lets a two-person crew hang a ten-metre wall in an afternoon. Tool-free locks, front-serviceable modules, hot-swappable power supplies and receiving cards keep the schedule when something fails at 22:00. Cabinets that share one frame across indoor and outdoor pitches let a rental house build any show from one inventory.\n\n> A rental wall is judged by how quickly it comes down, not only how good it looks up.\n\n## Curves, corners and floors\n\nTouring cabinets curve in locked steps — typically ±10° or ±15° between neighbours — so the same stock builds flat, concave and convex sets. Corner cabinets extend the system into right angles and cubes; load-bearing floor cabinets with anti-slip surfaces carry performers and dollies; transparent rental cabinets build see-through backdrops for concerts and sports. Plan the ground support or trussing for the final geometry, not the flat drawing.\n\n## Outdoor rental LED\n\nFestival and outdoor stages need IP65 cabinets, 4,000 to 5,000 nits, rear cable glands and wind-load calculations for the ground-support structure. Outdoor rental pitches of 3.9 to 5.9 mm serve most main stages; 7.8 to 10.4 mm suits side screens and delay towers viewed from far back.\n\n## Pre-show checks that matter\n\n1.   0 1 Rigging certificates and load calculation per truss point, with secondary safeties fitted.\n2.   0 2 Brightness and colour calibration applied on site with the show lighting, not only at the factory.\n3.   0 3 Refresh rate above 3,840 Hz — 7,680 Hz for broadcast — for camera-facing shows.\n4.   0 4 Redundant data loop confirmed by pulling a cable during line check.\n5.   0 5 IP rating, cable glands and drip loops for outdoor dates.\n6.   0 6 Spares on the truck: 5% of the wall in cabinets, modules, power supplies and receiving cards.\n\n## Rental versus purchase\n\nEvents under a few weeks a year rent; venues, studios and houses of worship with year-round use buy. A rental-grade cabinet bought for permanent installation costs more than a fixed-install product but keeps its resale value and tolerates reconfiguration. For touring productions, dry-hire from a rental house with matching inventory avoids mixed batches and colour differences on stage.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\nHow long does it take to build a rental LED wall?\nA two-person crew hangs about 20–30 m² of lightweight rental cabinets per hour, so a 10 × 5 m stage screen goes up in an afternoon including line check.\n\nWhat pixel pitch is used for stage LED screens?\nIndoor stages use 2.6–3.9 mm; outdoor main stages 3.9–5.9 mm; side screens and delay towers 7.8–10.4 mm.\n\nCan rental LED screens be used outdoors?\nYes, with IP65-rated cabinets, 4,000–5,000 nits brightness and a wind-load-calculated ground support or trussing.\n\nWhat refresh rate is needed for LED on camera?\nAt least 3,840 Hz for corporate and concert video, and 7,680 Hz or higher for broadcast and film.",
