    "dateFr": "10 août 2026",
    "publishedTimeIso": "2026-08-05T14:15:00.000Z",
    "content": "Corporate environments are built from proportion, material and light. An LED display that ignores those rules — a black rectangle bolted to marble — reads as an afterthought no matter how fine its pitch. A display that follows them reads as intent. This article covers how to design corporate LED walls for lobbies, boardrooms, briefing centres and open offices: the format decisions, the technical specifications that matter, and the content strategy that turns a screen into a statement of company identity.\n\n## The screen is a surface, not a device\n\nThe strongest corporate installations treat the LED surface as a material choice alongside stone, glass and timber. That means edge-to-edge coverage with no visible bezel, flush mounting inside the wall plane, and content authored for the exact aspect ratio of the architecture rather than a 16:9 file stretched across an ultra-wide surface. Once the display is planned as a surface, the choice of pitch, cabinet depth and brightness becomes a question of where people stand and how the room is lit.\n\n## Three decisions that define a corporate LED wall\n\n### Proportion\n\nSize the display to the architecture, not to a television standard. Ultra-wide, portrait and wrapped formats are natural in LED because cabinets tile in any direction. A 9:1 ribbon above a reception desk or a 2:3 portrait panel beside a lift core often communicates more than a large 16:9 rectangle.\n\n### Depth\n\nSub-30 mm cabinets allow true flush mounting; the screen disappears into the wall plane and the surrounding finish runs up to its edge. Deeper cabinets need a recess or a build-out, which is where installations start to look added-on.\n\n### Content\n\nCommission motion design for the space. Corporate identity in light behaves differently from identity in print: slow, high-contrast, generative or abstract pieces read better across a lobby than a corporate video with captions. Plan a content calendar the way a gallery plans exhibitions.\n\n> A well-designed lobby screen never looks switched on. It looks built in.\n\n## Lobby LED walls: brightness, pitch and viewing distance\n\nLobbies are typically daylit through glass façades, which means a lobby LED wall needs more brightness headroom than a boardroom — 1,000 to 1,500 nits calibrated down to ambient — with automatic dimming by time of day. Viewing distance ranges from two metres at reception to twenty at the entrance, so 1.5 to 1.9 mm is the usual pitch: fine enough for visitors who walk up to it, efficient enough for a wall of 20 m² or more.\n\n## Boardroom and briefing-room LED displays\n\nHere the display is functional: spreadsheets, video calls, live dashboards and presentations. Fine pitch of 0.9 to 1.5 mm, calibrated uniform brightness and a refresh rate above 3,840 Hz for camera-facing rooms are non-negotiable. Front serviceability keeps maintenance out of working hours and off the ceiling. Look for cabinets that pass EMC Class B for offices and for processors that accept multiple inputs with picture-in-picture without a separate matrix switcher.\n\n### All-in-one LED for meeting rooms\n\nFor rooms between 20 and 60 m², all-in-one LED displays of 108 to 136 inches replace projector and screen with a single wall-mounted unit at 1.25 to 1.56 mm pitch, integrated processing and a wireless-presentation module. They install in a day and remove the alignment and calibration work of a bespoke wall.\n\n## What to specify in a corporate LED tender\n\n1.   0 1 Pixel pitch matched to the closest viewing position (see our pixel-pitch guide).\n2.   0 2 Cabinet depth under 30 mm for flush mounting, with front or rear service access stated.\n3.   0 3 Brightness of 600–1,500 nits with automatic ambient dimming and a per-cabinet calibration report.\n4.   0 4 Refresh rate above 3,840 Hz and grey-scale depth of 14 bits or more for camera use.\n5.   0 5 Processor inputs, redundancy (dual power, loop-through data) and remote monitoring.\n6.   0 6 Content production: who authors it, in what format and how often it changes.\n\n## Operating costs and lifespan\n\nA calibrated fine-pitch wall running at 30–40% brightness in an office draws far less than its peak rating and reaches 100,000 hours of LED life. Energy-efficient LED platforms — common-cathode driving and low-power packages — reduce running cost and heat, which in turn removes the need for extra cooling in a sealed lobby.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\nWhat pixel pitch should a corporate lobby LED wall have?\nMost lobby walls use 1.5–1.9 mm. Go finer (1.2 mm) only if visitors regularly stand within two metres or the wall shows text; go coarser (2.5 mm) for very large walls viewed from across an atrium.\n\nHow thin can an LED wall be for flush mounting?\nModern fine-pitch cabinets are 25–40 mm deep. Under 30 mm the wall can be recessed flush with a plasterboard or stone finish.\n\nLED wall or LCD video wall for a boardroom?\nLED has no bezels, higher brightness and longer life; LCD is cheaper below three metres wide. For rooms that host video calls, LED with a refresh rate above 3,840 Hz avoids flicker on camera.\n\nHow much does a corporate LED wall cost to run?\nA 10 m² fine-pitch wall at typical office brightness draws roughly 1–1.5 kW, far below its peak rating. Automatic dimming and efficient LED platforms cut that further.",
