    "slug": "outdoor-brightness",
    "img": "/uploads/site/ins-outdoor-brightness.jpg",
    "category": "DOOH",
    "chip": "DOOH",
    "title": "Outdoor LED brightness explained: nits, contrast and content for DOOH screens in direct sunlight",
    "titleFr": "Luminosité LED extérieure expliquée : nits, contraste et contenu pour les écrans DOOH en plein soleil",
    "read": "8 MIN READ",
    "date": "08 AUG 2026",
    "dateFr": "8 août 2026",
    "publishedTimeIso": "2026-08-01T10:00:00.000Z",
    "content": "Ask what makes an outdoor LED display succeed and most people answer resolution. It is not. The specification that wins the fight against daylight is brightness measured in nits, the contrast that keeps dark content dark under a bright sky, and the way the content itself is graded for the street. This guide covers outdoor LED brightness levels, the difference between brightness and contrast, thermal and IP requirements, and practical content rules for digital out-of-home (DOOH) screens and media façades.\n\n## Daylight is 100,000 lux\n\nA bright interior is around 500 lux; an overcast day outdoors is 10,000; direct sunlight on a façade exceeds 100,000. An indoor display at 1,000 nits simply vanishes in that light. Serious outdoor DOOH surfaces run 5,000 to 10,000 nits, and the difference between the two is the difference between legible at noon and legible at dusk. A south-facing screen with the sun behind the viewer needs the top of that range; a north-facing screen under a canopy can live at the bottom.\n\n### How many nits does an outdoor LED screen need?\n\n1.   0 1 Shaded or north-facing, under canopy: 4,000–5,000 nits.\n2.   0 2 Open street, mixed orientation: 5,500–7,000 nits.\n3.   0 3 South- or west-facing façade in full sun: 8,000–10,000 nits.\n4.   0 4 Transparent mesh on glass (light passes through): 5,000–10,000 nits with high transparency.\n\n## Contrast is what the eye actually reads\n\nBrightness gets attention; contrast delivers the message. Black-face LEDs, louvered masks and matte surface treatments reduce reflected sunlight so that dark content stays dark. A 7,000-nit display with a shiny, grey surface reads worse at noon than a 5,000-nit display with deep blacks. When comparing outdoor LED products, ask for the contrast ratio measured in sunlight, not only the peak brightness on the datasheet.\n\n> Outdoors, the sun is your competitor for every pixel. Design the content to win.\n\n## Grading DOOH content for the street\n\n1.   0 1 Favour high-contrast compositions: light type on dark fields or the reverse, never mid-tones on mid-tones.\n2.   0 2 Avoid fine detail below 3% of screen height; at 30 m it disappears.\n3.   0 3 Use motion sparingly and slowly: passing viewers see a frame, not a film.\n4.   0 4 Keep one message per frame and hold each frame for at least six seconds.\n5.   0 5 Schedule brightness: full output at noon, 30–40% after dark to protect neighbours, comply with local light regulations and extend LED lifespan.\n\n## The engineering behind high-brightness LED\n\nHigh brightness generates heat. Outdoor cabinets need sealed IP65 construction front and rear, efficient thermal paths from the LED board to the cabinet skin, and drivers rated for continuous duty between −20 and +50 °C. Energy-efficient LED platforms lower running cost — on a 100 m² façade, a 16% efficiency gain is measured in thousands of kilowatt-hours a year. Look for front-serviceable modules on façades where rear access is impossible, and for cabinets that curve for architectural corners.\n\n### Pixel pitch for outdoor displays\n\nBecause viewing distances start at ten metres, 6.9 to 10.4 mm is the correct range for most street-level DOOH. Finer pitches (3.9–4.8 mm) belong on near-field surfaces such as shop fronts, bus shelters and building entrances. See our pixel-pitch guide for the calculation.\n\n## Ambient light sensors and brightness scheduling\n\nAn outdoor screen should never run at one brightness all day. A light sensor on the processor adjusts output continuously, while a schedule caps night-time brightness. This protects viewers, satisfies planning authorities and, because LED ageing accelerates with drive current and heat, keeps the wall uniform for years longer.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\nHow many nits do I need for an outdoor LED display?\nPlan for 5,000–7,000 nits on typical street-facing screens and 8,000–10,000 nits on façades in direct sun. Anything below 4,000 nits will wash out at midday.\n\nIs brightness or contrast more important for outdoor LED?\nBoth, but contrast is often the deciding factor. Black-face LEDs and matte masks keep dark content dark in sunlight, so a 5,000-nit high-contrast screen can outperform a brighter, glossier one.\n\nWhat IP rating does an outdoor LED screen need?\nIP65 on both front and rear is the standard for permanent outdoor installation; IP54 rear is acceptable only in fully sheltered positions.\n\nDo outdoor LED screens need to dim at night?\nYes. Night-time output at 30–40% avoids glare complaints, complies with most municipal light rules and slows LED ageing.",
